course: books
lesson: 87
title: Just So Stories, How the Alphabet Was Made, part 1: a carp-fish's mouth, a tail, an egg and a snake
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: A week after the picture-letter muddle, Taffy slips off to go carp-fishing with her Daddy again instead of helping Mummy hang hides. She giggles about the Head Chief's puffed cheeks and the mud in the Stranger-man's hair; Daddy says he had to pay the Stranger-man two soft deerskins to say sorry.
explain: Over lunch Taffy has «a secret surprise» idea. She asks Daddy to make a noise. He says «Ah!», and she thinks he looks like a carp-fish with its mouth open, so she scratches a carp's open mouth on bark: that picture will mean the sound ah.
explain: Daddy shows her how to make the drawing simpler and clearer, with the feeler across the mouth. Then they find a picture for each new noise: a carp's tail for the yer-sound in «Yah!», a round egg or stone for «Oh!», and a snake for «Ssh!», because it is the noise a snake makes.
explain: Taffy sees that the snake sign could mean «Daddy is thinking, come in quietly». Tegumai's eyes shine: he thinks his daughter has found the biggest secret of the world, better even than shark's teeth instead of flints.
fact: This story follows straight on from the last one: «The week after Taffimai Metallumai ... made that little mistake about her Daddy's spear and the Stranger-man and the picture-letter».
fact: Tegumai says: «I had to pay two deerskins, soft ones with fringes, to the Stranger-man for the things we did to him.» Taffy: «We didn't do anything», it was Mummy and the other Neolithic ladies, and the mud.
fact: Taffy says «inciting» and «incitement» when she means exciting and excitement, «s'prise» for surprise and «thinked» for thought; these are her funny child's words.
fact: Taffy «sat mousy-quiet for ten whole minutes» while her Daddy scratched on birch-bark with a shark's tooth.
fact: Taffy plans to draw the carp-mouth in the smoke at the back of the Cave, as a surprise for Daddy.
fact: Picture 1: Taffy draws only the open mouth of a carp-fish «standing on their heads rooting in the mud», and it means the sound ah.
fact: Tegumai adds «the feeler that hangs across his mouth», because «the perches and trouts haven't got feelers», so everyone will know it is a carp.
fact: For «Yah!» Taffy says it is «a mixy noise»; they draw the carp's tail, separately, for the yer-part: «If they're joined, I'll forget.»
fact: For «Oh!» they draw a round shape like an egg or a stone, because your mouth is round when you say it.
fact: For «Ssh!» Taffy draws a snake, «the noise a snake makes, Daddy, when it is thinking and doesn't want to be disturbed».
fact: Tegumai: «I believe we've found out the big secret of the world.» The book has small pictures of each sign, numbered (1.), (2.) and so on.
fact: Real life: the idea in this story is close to the truth. About 3,800 years ago, workers who spoke a language related to Hebrew and Arabic, living in or near Egypt, took Egyptian picture-signs and used each one only for the FIRST sound of its name.
fact: Real life: our letter A began as a picture of an ox's head, called «alp» (ox); turn A upside down and you can still see the head and horns.
fact: Real life: our letter O began as a picture of an eye, a round shape, not an egg; Kipling's egg is not so far off.
fact: Talk in this lesson: «highkey» = very, openly; «nesh» (North and Midlands England) = feels the cold easily; «lekker» (South Africa) = nice, great.
exercise: What did Taffy's Mummy want her to do instead of going fishing? | Help hang up hides on the drying-poles | Fetch water from the spring | Look after the baby | Pick berries in the wood behind the Cave
exercise: Why does Taffy giggle? | She remembers the Head Chief's puffed cheeks and the Stranger-man's muddy hair | Daddy fell in the river | A carp tickled her toes while she paddled in the shallow water | She is thinking of the beavers
exercise: What did Tegumai have to pay the Stranger-man? | Two soft deerskins with fringes | A new spear with a black handle, the best in the Tribe | Ten carp | Nothing at all
exercise: Taffy says «We didn't do anything». Who does she say it was? | Mummy and the other Neolithic ladies, and the mud | The Head Chief, who puffed out his cheeks and shouted | The beavers | Daddy
exercise: What does Taffy do for ten whole minutes over lunch? | Sits mousy-quiet with a marrow-bone | Swims in the river | Draws beavers | Sleeps on the warm bank in the sunshine
exercise: What noise does Tegumai make first? | «Ah!» | «Ooh!» | «Ssh!» | «Yah!»
exercise: Why does Taffy say he looks like a carp-fish? | His mouth is wide open | He is wet from the river | He is wriggling | He has a feeler on his face, just like a carp
exercise: What does Taffy draw for the sound ah? | A carp-fish's open mouth | A whole carp-fish, from its head to its tail | An egg with a crack in it | A snake curled up in a ring
exercise: What does Tegumai add to her drawing? | The feeler that hangs across the carp's mouth | Its eyes | Its tail | Some water and weed around it, to show where it lives
exercise: Why does the feeler help? | Perches and trouts haven't got feelers, so you'll know it is a carp | It makes the picture prettier and longer | It shows the fish is angry and about to bite somebody's toe | It shows which way is up
exercise: Where does Taffy want to draw the carp-mouth to surprise Daddy? | In the smoke at the back of the Cave | On the river bank | On Daddy's back | On the drying-poles, among the hides
exercise: What does Taffy call the noise «Yah!»? | A mixy noise | A rude noise | A carp noise | A snake noise
exercise: Why does Taffy want the tail drawn separately, not joined? | «If they're joined, I'll forget» | Because she can't draw joined things | Because Daddy told her to | Because the tail is too big
exercise: Why does the tail come first in a carp standing on its head? | Carp root in the mud head-down, so the tail sticks up first | Carp always swim backwards in the Wagai river when it rains | The tail is bigger | Taffy drew it first by mistake
exercise: What picture do they choose for «Oh!»? | Something round, like an egg or a stone | A carp's tail, turned upside down | A snake | A hill
exercise: Why does an egg or stone suit «Oh!»? | You make your mouth all round to say it | Eggs say «Oh» when they crack open on a hot stone | Stones are heavy | Daddy likes eggs
exercise: What did Tegumai really mean by «Ssh!»? | He was thinking and didn't want to be disturbed | He heard a big fish coming up the river | He wanted Taffy to sing | He was angry with her
exercise: What does Taffy draw for the ssh-noise? | A snake | A finger on lips | A carp-fish | A beaver
exercise: What will a snake drawn by the back-cave door tell Taffy? | Daddy is thinking hard, so come in mousy-quiet | Watch out, there is a real snake inside the cave | Dinner is ready | Daddy has gone fishing
exercise: What does Tegumai say they have found? | The big secret of the world | A new kind of fish | A cure for colds and fevers from swamp-water | A way to catch beavers
exercise: Which earlier Tribe invention does Tegumai compare it with? | Using shark's teeth instead of flints for spear-heads | Making fire | The birch-bark canoe that carried them down the Wagai | The Tribal Drums
exercise: Looking back: in the last story, which spear did Taffy want fetched? | Daddy's black-handled spear | The broken spear | The Head Chief's spear | A new spear from the Stranger-man
test: Ollie: «this is a new story, Taffy's never been in the book before, highkey sure.» (highkey = very, openly) Put it right. | Wrong: it is the week after the picture-letter muddle of the last story | Right, she is a brand-new girl in this story, with a different Daddy | She was in the Armadillo story, riding on the Tortoise | She was in the Whale story
test: Why does Taffy's idea work better than her picture-letter? | Each sign stands for one sound, so it can't be read as a battle | The pictures are bigger and drawn in colour this time | Daddy draws everything this time, and he draws much better | It is on stone
test: Tegumai says perches and trouts haven't got feelers. Why does that matter to a sign? | A sign must be clear and not get mixed up with others | Perches are bad to eat and the Tribe never draws them | Carp are cleverer than trout | It doesn't matter at all
test: Mia, a bit nesh (feels the cold): «why'd she make the tail a separate sign? join them, it's quicker.» What does Taffy say? | If they're joined she'll forget which sound is which | Joined signs are too heavy | Daddy forbids it | The tail belongs to a different fish, a trout
test: What would happen if every sound had no fixed picture? | Nobody could be sure how to read the signs | Writing would be faster and much easier for everyone | Everyone would understand better | Fish would talk
test: Which is TRUE about this part? | Tegumai is truly excited and attends to Taffy's game | Tegumai is cross and refuses to play | Mummy comes down to the river and helps with the drawings | They catch many carp
test: Real life: how did our letter A begin? | As a picture of an ox's head | As a carp's mouth | As a ladder with two legs | As a tent
test: Real life: what was the clever idea of the first alphabet-makers, about 3,800 years ago? | Each picture stood only for the first sound of its name | Each picture showed a whole story | They used numbers only | They drew only animals, one for every word in their language
test: Real life: where did our letter O come from? | A picture of an eye | A picture of a round egg | A picture of a gold ring | The round sun rising over the sea
test: Looking back: what did the Stranger-man give Taffy to show he meant no harm? | A piece of birch-bark | A shark's tooth | A carp | A deerskin
test: Looking back: what did the Head Chief say the next picture-letter needed? | Someone who could talk their language, to explain it | More beavers | Bigger bark, so the spear would fit | A drum
test: Looking back: how many letters did the Head Chief foretell? | Twenty-six | Exactly twenty-two | Just five letters | One hundred
test: Looking back: in the last story, Taffy borrowed a shark's tooth to draw. What does Tegumai use to scratch on bark here? | A shark's tooth | A sharp grey flint | A burnt stick from the fire | His muddy finger
test: Zoe from Cape Town: «lekker, so Daddy was annoyed by the game?» (lekker = great) Put it right. | No: he was «quite incited too», his eyes shining with excitement | Yes, he told her to go home and help Mummy with the hides | Yes, he threw the bark into the river | He fell asleep
test: ★★★ Taffy says «inciting» for exciting. Which other word does she bend the same way in this part? | «Incitement» for excitement | «Feeler» for finger | «Carp» for fish | «Egg» for the round stone by the river
test: ★★★ Counting: in this part they make signs for how many different sounds? | Four: ah, yer, oh, ssh | Three: ah, oh, ssh | Two: ah and oh | Five: ah, yer, oh, ssh and sho
test: ★★★ Careful: which sign did Tegumai draw first, the very first time, for ah? | None: Taffy drew the first ah-sign, then Daddy drew a better one | The egg | The snake | The carp's tail, with the sticky-down line where it joins
exam: Identify the sound for which the open mouth of a carp-fish was adopted as a sign. | Ah | Oh | Ssh | Yer
exam: Ascertain the reason advanced by Tegumai for the inclusion of a feeler across the mouth of the carp. | That perches and trouts possess no feelers, so the carp may not be mistaken | That the feeler renders the drawing more beautiful | That the Head Chief had so commanded at the assembly of the Tribe | That the feeler denotes the sound oh
exam: It is given that Tegumai uttered «Ssh!» while thinking. Select the response most consistent with the sign adopted. | A snake, being the creature that makes such a noise | An egg, being round | A carp's tail, the fish being presumed to stand upon its head | A spear
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the origin of the letter A in the real world? | It derives from an ancient picture of the head of an ox | It derives from the mouth of a carp-fish drawn in Surrey | It was devised by the Romans as a picture of a tent | It has no known origin
